  </div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Why the northern suburbs carry so much of it</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Corio and Norlane were built by industry, in the decades when asbestos cement was the cheap default. Ford's Norlane complex went up between 1925 and 1942 and was extended eastwards through the war years, and by the company's fiftieth anniversary in 1953 Ford was Geelong's biggest employer with more than 5,000 workers. The Shell refinery at Corio followed in 1956. International Harvester and a phosphate works drew more families again, and the Housing Commission built the estates that housed them.</p><p>The numbers make the point on their own. Norlane's Commission program peaked between 1951 and 1957, with more than 1,200 prefabricated houses imported from Holland and France, and ran to 2,464 houses by 1976. Corio held 857 people at the 1947 census and 15,342 by 2001. Asbestos cement was in normal residential use from the mid 1940s until the late 1980s, and the total Australian ban on manufacture, supply and use only came into force on 31 December 2003.</p><p>What nobody can tell you is how many of those houses actually contain it. The national estimate is that asbestos is still present in one in three homes. There is no published figure for Geelong or the Barwon region, and anyone who quotes you one has made it up. A <a href="/asbestos-removal-geelong/">house built or extended before 1990</a> is worth checking, and that is about as far as the age of a building can take you.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">What you are allowed to do yourself, and the rental catch</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>Victoria's OHS Regulations cap unlicensed removal at 10 square metres of non-friable material, and at no more than one hour of removal work in any seven day period. Both caps have to hold, and the hour adds up across jobs rather than resetting each time. That is the limit the law sets for anyone doing this as work, and it is the honest reason a full fence line, a garage or a house roof goes to a licensed removalist. All three are far past both caps.</p><p>There is a nuance that most asbestos content online gets wrong. Those regulations bind employers and self-employed people at workplaces, and WorkSafe's own compliance code says they do not apply to someone removing asbestos at their own home, because a home is not a workplace. WorkSafe still recommends homeowners follow the same code. The catch matters here, in suburbs where so much of the stock began as Housing Commission housing: the moment a tradesperson, a tenant, a rental property, a business premises or a paid helper is involved, those caps stop being a recommendation and become law.</p><p>Friable material never qualifies for that exemption at any quantity and always needs a Class A removalist. The rules are also why a licensed removalist often cannot start tomorrow. The licence holder has to notify WorkSafe before the work begins, at least 24 hours out for jobs of 10 square metres or less of non-friable material, and at least five days out for anything larger. That duty sits with them, never with you.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:0 24px;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto 56px;">
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 18px;">Where it can go, and what the job is likely to cost</h2>
    <div class="rich"><p>EPA Victoria's register of landfills licensed for asbestos waste lists 26 sites across the state, and exactly one of them is in the Barwon region: Drysdale Landfill, run by the City of Greater Geelong, which takes small domestic quantities up to a 6x4 trailer load. Fees apply and the council does not publish the figure, so ring (03) 5251 2935 and ask before you load anything. No other Geelong area site appears on that register, so do not assume your nearest transfer station will take it. The <a href="/asbestos-removal-drysdale/">Drysdale page</a> covers that side of the region in more detail.</p><p>On price, the most useful number is the minimum job charge, published at $800 to $1,500. Nearly every residential enquiry is a small job, and that minimum is why pulling four sheets off a shed does not cost four sheets' worth. Beyond that, general removal runs $45 to $150 per square metre, a whole fence $1,000 to $3,500, a house roof $4,000 to $15,000, and a clearance certificate $165 to $250 where one is needed. Our full <a href="/asbestos-removal-cost/">cost guide</a> sets out where each of those figures comes from.</p><p>Two honest caveats on all of that. Every published Victorian figure is a Melbourne metro benchmark, because no Geelong or Barwon provider publishes rates at all, and two Melbourne suppliers price regional work separately. GST treatment also varies between providers, so ask each removalist whether their quote includes it. Prices stay indicative until somebody has looked at the actual material.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>
<section style="padding:72px 24px;background:#EAF5FB;">
  <div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">FAQ</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(24px,3.5vw,36px);margin:0 0 32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:16px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Is my Corio or Norlane house old enough to have asbestos in it?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Probably old enough to be worth checking. Asbestos cement was in normal residential use from the mid 1940s until the late 1980s, and the Corio and Norlane estates were built squarely inside that window, mostly from the early 1950s onwards. Age is not a test though. The only way to know is a sample analysed by a NATA accredited laboratory.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Where can I take asbestos from Lara or Corio?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">Drysdale Landfill is the only facility in the Barwon region on EPA Victoria's register of landfills licensed to receive asbestos waste. It accepts small domestic quantities only, capped at a 6x4 trailer, fees apply, and you should ring (03) 5251 2935 first, because an authorised landfill can still decline a load or ask you to book a time. It can never go in a general skip bin or a council hard waste collection.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Can I pull down the old fibro fence at my own place in Lara myself?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">At your own home the OHS Regulations do not technically apply, because a home is not a workplace, but WorkSafe recommends homeowners follow the same code anyway. That code caps the work at 10 square metres of non-friable material and one hour in any seven days, and a full fence line blows past both. If the property is rented, or a tradie or a paid helper touches it, those caps are law rather than advice. Get it tested first, then get a quote.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #e3ded4;border-radius:8px;padding:24px 28px;">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Nunito Sans',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#0A3D5C;margin:0 0 10px;">Who has to get the clearance certificate after the work is done?</h3>
        <p style="font-size:15px;line-height:1.75;color:#555;margin:0;">For a home where the owner engaged the removalist directly, the duty to obtain the clearance certificate sits with the licence holder, not with you. It has to come from an independent person who is not the removalist and has no stake in the job, which is why it appears as a separate line on a quote, usually $165 to $250. A certificate is not required at all if the work involved only non-friable material and did not exceed 10 square metres in total.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
